<tr><td align=3D"left" class=3D"text" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size=
:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style=3D"font-family:Georg=
ia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;c=
olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Away from the pitch, the most fascinating =
aspect of the match was the manner in which it was covered, specifically by=
 a network that has spent decades insisting the now irreversibly tarnished =
product on which it spends and makes billions is The Best League In The Wor=
ld=E2=84=A2. Perhaps having witnessed Joe Cole desperately mugging on City=
=E2=80=99s guilty verdict the previous day on TNT Sports, Sky had assembled=
 its Heavy Artillery O=E2=80=99Punditry in Jamie Carragher, Gary Neville, R=
oy Keane, Micah Richards and Daniel Sturridge. Each of them made a reasonab=
ly decent fist of toeing the fine line between condemning City for their ch=
eating, without making the obvious observation that, for the past 17 years,=
 their employers have been selling subscribers what many have long suspecte=
d was a massive lie.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CIf you could take me off Manchest=
er City games moving forward for the next few months until the verdicts hav=
e been heard, I would be absolutely delighted,=E2=80=9D parped Neville. =E2=
=80=9CAt the end of the game it is quite hard because you are imagining, ba=
sically, if these guilty verdicts are confirmed, there is going to be a hug=
e cloud hanging over the whole league.=E2=80=9D Obviously, the irritating p=
edant in Football Daily behoves us to clarify that the guilty verdict has a=
lready been confirmed, but is now simply subject to appeal. In the meantime=
, we are left watching a multibillion-pound mirage where every match featur=
ing City feels like that McFly family photo in Back to the Future, with var=
ious games, goals, trophy lifts and victory parades fading in and out of ex=
istence until we get a final, definitive decree.</p>
